    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ask ten homeowners why they are sealing an epoxy floor and nine will say "to keep water out." Epoxy already keeps water out. The clear coat exists for three completely different reasons: it stops sunlight from yellowing the resin, it takes the abrasion that would otherwise wear through your color, and on a flake floor it is the layer that locks the chips down and gives you a smooth surface instead of a sheet of sandpaper.
        </p>

        <div id="why-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Seal Epoxy at All</h2>
          <p>
            Standard two-part epoxy is an aromatic resin. Aromatic chemistry is inexpensive, bonds beautifully to concrete, and builds thickness in a single pass — and it ambers under ultraviolet light. A garage with a south-facing door will show it within a year or two: the strip of floor inside the door goes noticeably warmer in tone than the rest, and a gray floor starts reading as a dirty beige. No cleaning fixes it, because the color change is in the resin itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            A clear aliphatic topcoat is the fix. Aliphatic urethanes and polyaspartics are UV-stable by design, so they keep their water-clear appearance and shield the epoxy underneath from the light that would discolor it. That alone justifies the coat on any garage where the door spends time open.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second reason is mechanical. Cured epoxy is hard but relatively soft against dragged steel and grit, and it is notoriously vulnerable to hot tire pickup — the phenomenon where a warm tire's plasticizers bond to the coating and pull it up when the car moves. Urethane and polyaspartic topcoats are dramatically more resistant to both. In practice a topcoated floor keeps its gloss in the wheel paths for years longer than a bare epoxy one, and when it does finally wear, the wear is in a sacrificial layer that costs a few hundred dollars to replace.
          </p>
          <p>
            The third reason applies to decorative floors. On a full-broadcast flake system, the chips are only half-embedded in the base coat. Without one or two clear coats over them the surface is coarse enough to shred a knee and impossible to mop. The topcoat fills between the flakes and produces the smooth, deep finish people associate with a professional install.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. When to Seal or Recoat</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">On a New Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Apply the clear coat inside the manufacturer's recoat window, which is typically 12 to 24 hours after the base coat at around 70 degrees. Inside that window the base coat is still chemically active and the two layers bond together as one. It is the strongest bond you will ever get, and it requires zero sanding.
          </p>
          <p>
            Miss the window and the job changes completely. A fully cured epoxy surface is too slick and too inert for a new coat to grip, so you have to create a mechanical key by abrading the whole floor. That is another half day of work with a rented buffer, which is why professional crews plan the whole install around hitting the recoat window rather than around convenience.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">On an Existing Floor</h3>
          <p>
            The clearest signal for recoating is uneven gloss: the tire paths have gone matte while the perimeter still reflects the lights. That means the clear coat has thinned where the traffic is, and there may be very little left over the color. Other signals are a floor that no longer beads water, tire marks that no longer wash off the way they used to, and fine scratching visible under raking light.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recoat while the pigmented layer is still intact. Once wear reaches the color, you are into a full restoration — grinding the old system off and starting over, at roughly ten times the cost of a clear coat refresh. Most residential garages hit this point somewhere between five and eight years, and the timing depends far more on how much grit is on the floor than on which product was originally used.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Do Not Seal Over These Problems</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A clear coat is not a repair. If the existing coating is delaminating, bubbling, or lifting at the edges, sealing over it locks the failure in and adds cost to the eventual removal. The same goes for moisture blistering from a slab with no vapor barrier, which will keep pushing regardless of what you put on top. Fix adhesion and moisture problems first, or grind and start again.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Topcoat Chemistry Compared</h2>
          <p>
            There are four realistic choices, and the trade-off is almost always working time against durability.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[560px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Topcoat</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">UV Stable</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pot Life</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Expected Life</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear epoxy</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — ambers</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30-45 min</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based urethane</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part aliphatic urethane</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">45-90 min</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-10 yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyaspartic</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-30 min</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-15 yr</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Two-part aliphatic urethane</strong> is the sensible default for a homeowner doing this themselves. It is genuinely durable, UV-stable, forgiving enough to keep a wet edge across a two-car bay, and widely available as part of a manufacturer's system so compatibility is not a guess.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Polyaspartic</strong> is the best-performing option and the least forgiving. Pot life can be fifteen minutes in a warm garage, it can be walked on in a handful of hours, and the whole floor can go down in a day. That speed is why the one-day coating companies use it. It is also why a first-timer working alone often ends up with roller marks and a ridge where the material set before the next pass arrived. If you use it, mix small batches, work with a partner, and keep the garage cool.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clear epoxy</strong> is what comes in most big-box kits, and it is the weakest topcoat of the four. It ambers, it scratches, and it does little for hot tire pickup because it is the same chemistry as the layer below. It is acceptable indoors in a garage that rarely sees direct sun, and it is a poor investment anywhere else. For a full comparison of these systems as complete floors, see our guide to <a href="/epoxy-vs-polyaspartic-garage-floor/">epoxy versus polyaspartic garage floors</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Surface Prep and Keying</h2>
          <p>
            Every topcoat failure traces back to prep. Clear coats are thin and unforgiving, and they will not bond through contamination that a heavy build coat might have tolerated.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Degrease First, Sand Second</h3>
          <p>
            Sanding a contaminated floor grinds the oil into the scratch pattern you just created, which is the worst possible outcome. Clean the floor completely first with a water-based degreaser and rinse it thoroughly — our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-epoxy-garage-floor/">cleaning an epoxy garage floor</a> covers the products that rinse clean without leaving film. Let it dry fully before any abrasion.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Abrade to a Uniform Dull</h3>
          <p>
            Rent a floor buffer and fit an 80 to 120 grit sanding screen, or use a random orbital for a small area. The goal is not to remove material, it is to remove gloss. When the entire floor reads uniformly matte with no shiny islands left, the key is adequate. Shiny islands are exactly where the new coat will peel first, so chase them down with a pole sander before moving on.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Repair Chips and Cracks</h3>
          <p>
            Fill chips with a compatible epoxy patch or gel, feather the edges flush, and let them cure before topcoating. A clear coat over an unfilled chip magnifies it rather than hiding it, because the gloss around a divot draws the eye straight to it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vacuum, Then Tack</h3>
          <p>
            Vacuum the entire floor with a shop vac and a brush head, then go over it with a microfiber pad slightly damp with denatured alcohol. Sanding dust is the number one cause of a gritty finish in a clear coat, and it hides in the perimeter joint and around the door track. Do not blow the floor out with compressed air unless you intend to vacuum again afterward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Application, Step by Step</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Check Conditions</h3>
          <p>
            Aim for 60 to 85 degrees with relative humidity under about 75 percent, and confirm the slab temperature is at least five degrees above the dew point. Coating a cold slab on a humid day is how you get blush — a cloudy, sometimes sticky surface caused by moisture condensing into the curing film. Check the forecast for the following 24 hours too, not just the application window.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Mask and Set Up</h3>
          <p>
            Tape the base of the walls, mask the door track, and stage everything before mixing: roller frame on a pole, correct nap covers, a cut-in brush, mixing paddle, and a clean pail. Once a polyaspartic is mixed you have no time to go looking for a roller cover.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Mix Exactly</h3>
          <p>
            Two-component coatings are stoichiometric, not approximate. Measure by the ratio on the label, mix for the full time stated — usually three minutes on a drill paddle — and scrape the sides and bottom of the pail as you go. Unmixed resin from the corner of a bucket ends up as a permanently soft spot on the floor. Many products also require an induction period of a few minutes before use.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Cut In, Then Roll</h3>
          <p>
            Brush a band around the perimeter and any obstacles, then roll the field immediately so the cut-in stays wet. Work in strips that let you maintain a wet edge across the full width of the bay, rolling toward the door so you finish at the exit. Back-roll each strip in one direction for an even sheen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Keep It Thin</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step people get wrong. A clear coat is meant to go on at the coverage rate printed on the can, typically 400 to 600 square feet per gallon, and applying it heavy does not make it tougher. It makes it cure soft, trap solvent, and sometimes wrinkle. Two thin coats always beat one thick one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 6: Respect the Cure</h3>
          <p>
            Stay off it for the stated period — typically 12 to 24 hours to walk on, and considerably longer before parking. Driving on a topcoat that has not reached full hardness is the single fastest way to ruin a fresh job, and the damage looks exactly like hot tire pickup. Our guide to <a href="/garage-floor-epoxy-drying-time/">epoxy drying and curing times</a> covers what governs each stage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="antislip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Anti-Slip Additives and Sheen</h2>
          <p>
            A high-gloss clear coat on a garage floor is genuinely slippery when wet, and a car pulling in during a rainstorm brings a lot of water with it. Anti-slip additive solves this cheaply. Aluminum oxide gives the most aggressive texture and is the best choice for a ramp or a floor that gets snow; polymer or silica beads are gentler underfoot and easier to mop.
          </p>
          <p>
            There are two ways to apply it. Mixing the grit into the coating keeps it suspended and gives an even texture, but it settles in the pail and has to be stirred continuously. Broadcasting it into the wet first coat and then sealing over with a second, clean coat gives a more controlled finish and is what most professionals do. Either way, start with a quarter cup per gallon and test a small area — it is easy to add more and impossible to take texture back out.
          </p>
          <p>
            On sheen, gloss looks spectacular and shows every scratch and every dust speck. Satin hides wear, hides minor prep imperfections, and stays looking consistent much longer. For a garage that gets used as a garage rather than photographed, satin is usually the better call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Cost, Coverage and Time</h2>
          <p>
            A standard two-car garage is about 400 to 480 square feet, which at typical clear coat rates means one gallon per coat with a little margin. Budget for two coats if you want the finish to last.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[520px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Cost</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part urethane, 2 gal</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$180-320</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two coats, two-car garage</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyaspartic, 2 gal</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$250-450</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast cure, short pot life</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Buffer rental and screens</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-80</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Half-day rental is enough</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rollers, brushes, tape, grit</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-70</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buy solvent-resistant covers</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional recoat</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2-4 per sq ft</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Includes prep and two coats</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Time-wise, plan on a full weekend: a day for cleaning, abrading, vacuuming and the first coat, and the following morning for the second. If you are pricing a whole new floor rather than a refresh, our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/">epoxy garage floor cost guide</a> breaks the numbers down by system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to seal an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Epoxy itself is waterproof, so sealing is not about moisture — it is about UV stability, chemical resistance and abrasion. A clear urethane or polyaspartic topcoat stops the epoxy ambering in sunlight, resists hot tire pickup far better, and takes the wear so the colored layer never does. On a flake floor it is mandatory, because the topcoat is what encapsulates the flake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A two-component aliphatic polyurethane for most homeowners, or a polyaspartic if you want a one-day turnaround and can work fast. Clear epoxy is the cheapest option and the weakest choice for a topcoat because it ambers under UV and scratches more easily. Single-component water-based urethanes are a reasonable budget refresh but wear noticeably faster.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after epoxy can you apply a clear coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Within the recoat window given on the data sheet, typically 12 to 24 hours at 70 degrees. Coating inside that window gives a chemical bond with no sanding. Miss it and the epoxy has cured too far, so you must abrade the entire surface with an 80 to 120 grit screen to create a mechanical key first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should an epoxy floor be recoated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A residential garage typically needs a fresh clear coat every five to eight years, sooner in the wheel paths if the floor sees daily driving. The signal is gloss loss in the tire tracks while the rest of the floor still shines. Recoating at that point is a weekend job; waiting until the color layer wears through means a full grind and reinstall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put polyurethane over epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the standard professional build. Polyurethane over epoxy is the classic garage floor system: epoxy supplies adhesion and build, urethane supplies UV stability and abrasion resistance. Confirm compatibility within one manufacturer's system, and abrade the epoxy if you are outside its recoat window.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to seal an epoxy garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Materials for a two-car garage run roughly $150 to $400 depending on chemistry, since one gallon covers about 400 to 600 square feet as a clear coat. Add $40 to $80 for a rental floor buffer and screens. Professional recoating typically runs $2 to $4 per square foot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you add anti-slip grit to a clear coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and on a glossy garage floor you should. Aluminum oxide or polymer grit is broadcast into the wet topcoat or mixed into it at roughly a quarter to a half cup per gallon. It changes the feel underfoot slightly and makes the floor marginally harder to mop, and it is the difference between a safe floor and a skating rink when a wet car pulls in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Mistakes That Ruin a Clear Coat</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying It Too Thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heavy clear coats cure soft, trap solvent, and can wrinkle or stay tacky for weeks. Hit the published coverage rate and add a second coat instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Abrasion on a Cured Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh coating over glossy cured epoxy has nothing to hold onto. It looks perfect for a month and then peels in sheets under the tires.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing More Than You Can Roll</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyaspartic in a warm garage can gel in the pail in fifteen minutes. Mix in halves, and have the second batch ready rather than the whole gallon open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a Cold Slab on a Humid Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Condensation into a curing film causes blush and adhesion loss. Check that the slab is at least five degrees above the dew point before you open a can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Epoxy Topcoat Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A topcoat is what determines how an epoxy floor looks and wears for the next decade, and it is applied at the point in the project where patience is shortest.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the Recoat Window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A topcoat applied inside the manufacturer's window bonds chemically to the epoxy beneath it. Applied later, the bond is mechanical only, and the two layers can separate cleanly under a hot tyre. Read the recoat times before you plan the schedule, not after the base coat is down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Non-UV-Stable Topcoat on a Sunlit Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxy ambers under ultraviolet light, and a clear epoxy topcoat yellows just as the base does. In a garage where the door stands open, a polyaspartic or aliphatic urethane topcoat is what keeps the colour stable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Out the Anti-Slip Additive</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A glossy epoxy floor is very slippery with water or snowmelt on it, and a garage floor sees both every winter. Fine aggregate broadcast into the topcoat is nearly invisible and removes the problem. Skipping it is the most-regretted omission on a finished floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Over a Contaminated Base Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dust, footprints, and amine blush on the cured base coat all prevent the topcoat bonding. Clean the floor thoroughly and, where the base has fully cured, scuff-sand it before the topcoat goes on. A topcoat is only as good as the surface it lands on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

